\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Component: productP -- a FICTITIOUS substance, NOT A REAL ONE:
  the product: MW(A) + MW(B), Cp_liq(A) + Cp_liq(B).

  One of the four records of the process-safety teaching reaction
  A + B -> P in the inert solvent S (DEV.md 4c C60, second review).  Every
  number below is an AUTHOR-SET TEACHING ASSUMPTION or is DERIVED from
  those by the rule written beside it; none is a measurement and none may
  be cited as data.  There is no formula and no CAS number, because there
  are no elements behind it.

  WHY THE NUMBERS ARE COHERENT BY CONSTRUCTION -- the reason this record
  exists (the first version of the lesson ran on a real esterification
  whose acetic-acid record paired a monomer ideal-gas datum with a heat of
  vaporisation to the dimerised vapour, so its liquid heat of reaction was
  several times too large):

    * Antoine (author-set) -> Tb, the temperature where it gives 1.01325
      bar (derived);
    * HvapTb = R Tb^2 ln(10) B/(Tb + C)^2, the Clausius-Clapeyron latent
      heat of THIS Antoine at Tb (derived) -- one vapour, the same one the
      vapour pressure describes;
    * Tc = Tb/0.65 (derived; Guldberg's rule, Tb ~ 2/3 Tc) and omega from
      Pitzer's definition, -log10(Psat(0.7 Tc)/Pc) - 1, on this Antoine
      (derived; Pc author-set);
    * Cp_liquid constant (author-set); Cp_idealGas constant and DERIVED so
      that d(Hvap)/dT = Cp_ig - Cp_liq holds at Tb with the slope of the
      Watson latent heat the engine uses;
    * the formation datum is on a FICTITIOUS reference: no elements stand
      behind it, and only the DIFFERENCES between the four records carry
      meaning -- the heat of reaction, which is the only use any case here
      makes of it.  A, B and S carry round author-set values; P's is
      derived so that the LIQUID heat of reaction, priced the way the
      engine prices a liquid (formation datum - Watson Hvap(298.15 K) +
      Cp_liq (T - 298.15 K)), is -60.000 kJ per mol of B (to 0.01 J/mol);
    * Cp_liq(P) = Cp_liq(A) + Cp_liq(B), so the heat of reaction does not
      depend on temperature (Kirchhoff, dCp = 0) and the reactor's
      adiabatic energy balance, which reads one heat of reaction, is the
      exact first law on these records; and MW(P) = MW(A) + MW(B), so mass
      is conserved.

  The liquid is declared IDEAL in the case (Raoult, gamma = 1): an ideal
  mixture has no azeotrope, so its bubble point lies between the boiling
  points of the components it contains.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        productP;
CAS         00-00-0;              // none: not a real substance
reviewStatus unverified;          // fictitious: nothing to verify against; the
                                  //   derived values are recomputed from the
                                  //   author-set ones by the rules above

MW          180.0;                // g/mol      derived: MW(A) + MW(B), mass conserved
Tc          738.4;                // K          derived: Tb/0.65
Pc          30.0;                 // bar        author-set
omega       0.1460;               // -          derived: Pitzer, on the Antoine
Tb          479.99;               // K          derived: Antoine = 1.01325 bar
HvapTb      42239;                // J/mol      derived: Clausius-Clapeyron at Tb
Vliq        1.895e-4;             // m^3/mol    author-set (MW / 950 kg/m^3)

standardThermochemistry
{
    dHf_298   -627251.45;         // J/mol  DERIVED: liquid heat of reaction -60.000 kJ/mol (header)
    s_298     520.0;              // J/(mol K)  author-set; enters only the printed S_ig of a props run
}

vaporPressure
{
    model         Antoine;
    coefficients  (4.1235   1770.6   -50.0);   // log10 P_bar = A - B/(T + C), author-set
    Trange        (250  500);                  // defined by construction over this window
}

idealGasHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (237.9);                     // J/(mol K)  derived (header)
    Trange        (250  500);
}

liquidHeatCapacity
{
    model         polynomial;
    coefficients  (300.0);                     // J/(mol K)  derived: Cp(A) + Cp(B), dCp = 0
    Trange        (250  500);
}
